# Git behaviour for this repository.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DOES, in one line: it tells git to treat .ipynb notebooks as
# notebooks rather than as lines of JSON text.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS. A notebook is a JSON file with the code, the execution counts and
# the cell outputs all mixed together. Git does not know that, so by default it
# merges notebooks line by line. That is close to meaningless for JSON: a merge can
# "succeed" with no conflict reported and still leave you a notebook holding cells
# from two different versions, or quietly reinstate old code. This is the main way
# a dirty merge enters this repo.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# IMPORTANT: this file alone does nothing.
#
# It only says *which* tool handles notebooks. Each developer must also install
# those tools into their own clone, once:
#
#     conda activate snt_development
#     nbstripout --install       # teaches git to strip outputs on commit
#     nbdime config-git --enable # teaches git to diff/merge notebooks by cell
#
# Full instructions: dev/README.md → "One-time git setup for notebooks".
#
# If a developer skips that step, git silently falls back to its normal text
# behaviour. Nothing breaks and nothing errors — they just lose the protection.
# So do not assume a colleague's clone is set up; ask.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# 1. Strip outputs on the way into git (rules R1, R2).
#    Outputs are executed results — they contain real country health data and they
#    make diffs unreviewable. This filter removes them as the file is staged, so
#    the copy in git is clean even if the copy on your disk is not. Your local
#    notebook keeps its outputs; only what git stores is stripped.
*.ipynb filter=nbstripout

# 2. Diff notebooks cell by cell instead of line by line (nbdime).
#    Turns an unreadable JSON diff into "cell 4 changed, this line of R differs".
*.ipynb diff=jupyternotebook

# 3. Merge notebooks cell by cell instead of line by line (nbdime).
#    This is the line that prevents the dirty-merge class of bug. It cannot prevent
#    all of them: when two people edit the same cell, nbdime still reports a genuine
#    conflict. What it stops is git inventing a silently-wrong resolution.
#
#    HOUSE RULE when a notebook conflict does happen: never hand-edit the JSON to
#    resolve it. Take one side whole, then redo the change in the notebook editor.
#    See dev/README.md and CLAUDE.md → "Notebook hygiene".
*.ipynb merge=jupyternotebook

# Windows security sidecars, in case one is ever staged by accident. Marking them
# binary keeps them out of diffs; .gitignore should stop them arriving at all.
*:Zone.Identifier binary
